        <h2 id="cloud-support">1. Cloud Support Engineer</h2>

        <p>
            Cloud Support Engineers help organizations maintain cloud-based
            applications, servers, storage, and infrastructure. They troubleshoot
            technical issues, investigate service interruptions, and help users
            resolve cloud-related problems.
        </p>

        <p>
            Professionals in this field may work with Amazon Web Services (AWS),
            Microsoft Azure, or Google Cloud. Beginners can start by learning
            cloud fundamentals, networking, and Linux.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Cloud computing fundamentals</li>
                <li>AWS or Microsoft Azure basics</li>
                <li>Linux commands</li>
                <li>Networking and troubleshooting</li>
                <li>Monitoring and problem-solving</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="cyber-analyst">2. Cyber Security Analyst</h2>

        <p>
            Cyber Security Analysts help protect computer systems, networks,
            and organizational data from cyber threats. They review security
            alerts, investigate suspicious activity, identify vulnerabilities,
            and help implement security controls.
        </p>

        <p>
            Students interested in this career should build a strong foundation
            in networking, operating systems, security principles, and
            defensive security practices.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Network security fundamentals</li>
                <li>Security monitoring</li>
                <li>Vulnerability assessment</li>
                <li>Risk assessment</li>
                <li>Incident investigation</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="network-admin">3. Network Administrator</h2>

        <p>
            Network Administrators manage and maintain an organization's
            computer networks. Their responsibilities may include configuring
            routers and switches, monitoring network performance, managing
            connectivity, and resolving network problems.
        </p>

        <p>
            Networking is also an important foundation for cloud computing
            and cyber security. Learning routing, switching, IP addressing,
            and troubleshooting can help students prepare for entry-level
            networking roles.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>TCP/IP and subnetting</li>
                <li>Routing and switching</li>
                <li>CCNA-level networking concepts</li>
                <li>Network troubleshooting</li>
                <li>Basic Linux administration</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="cloud-admin">4. Cloud Administrator</h2>

        <p>
            Cloud Administrators help manage cloud infrastructure, virtual
            machines, storage, user accounts, and access permissions. They
            monitor cloud resources and help maintain availability, security,
            and efficient resource usage.
        </p>

        <p>
            Practical experience with a cloud platform, virtual machines,
            permissions, and cloud monitoring can be useful when preparing
            for this career.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>AWS or Azure administration</li>
                <li>Virtual machines and storage</li>
                <li>Identity and access management (IAM)</li>
                <li>Cloud monitoring</li>
                <li>Cloud security fundamentals</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="soc-analyst">5. SOC Analyst</h2>

        <p>
            A Security Operations Center (SOC) Analyst monitors security alerts,
            reviews system logs, investigates suspicious activities, and assists
            with responding to cyber incidents. SOC teams help organizations
            identify and investigate potential security threats.
        </p>

        <p>
            Beginners interested in this role should practise log analysis,
            understand common attack techniques, and learn how security
            monitoring tools work.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>SIEM tool fundamentals</li>
                <li>Log analysis</li>
                <li>Threat detection</li>
                <li>Incident response fundamentals</li>
                <li>Networking and operating systems</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="system-admin">6. System Administrator</h2>

        <p>
            System Administrators manage servers, operating systems, user
            accounts, and enterprise IT infrastructure. They help maintain
            system availability, apply updates, manage permissions, and
            troubleshoot technical issues.
        </p>

        <p>
            This career can be suitable for students interested in Windows
            Server, Linux, virtualization, and day-to-day IT infrastructure
            management.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Windows Server administration</li>
                <li>Linux administration</li>
                <li>Active Directory</li>
                <li>Virtualization</li>
                <li>System monitoring and troubleshooting</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="devops">7. DevOps Engineer</h2>

        <p>
            DevOps Engineers help automate software development, testing,
            deployment, and infrastructure management. They work with tools
            that improve collaboration between software development and
            IT operations teams.
        </p>

        <p>
            DevOps is a more advanced career path for many graduates.
            Practical experience in Linux, scripting, cloud services,
            and software deployment can be helpful before applying
            for these roles.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Linux and Git</li>
                <li>Basic scripting</li>
                <li>Docker fundamentals</li>
                <li>CI/CD pipelines</li>
                <li>Cloud platforms and automation</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="information-security">8. Information Security Specialist</h2>

        <p>
            Information Security Specialists help organizations protect
            information, applications, and IT systems. Their work may include
            security policy implementation, risk assessment, vulnerability
            management, and support for compliance requirements.
        </p>

        <p>
            The role requires an understanding of security controls,
            organizational risks, and methods for protecting confidential
            business information.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Information security principles</li>
                <li>Risk assessment</li>
                <li>Security policies and controls</li>
                <li>Vulnerability management</li>
                <li>Security auditing fundamentals</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="technical-support">9. Technical Support Engineer</h2>

        <p>
            Technical Support Engineers diagnose and resolve hardware,
            software, operating system, and networking issues. They help
            employees or customers restore services and use their computer
            systems effectively.
        </p>

        <p>
            This can be a practical starting point for fresh graduates.
            Working in technical support helps develop troubleshooting,
            communication, and real-world IT operations experience.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Computer hardware and software</li>
                <li>Windows operating systems</li>
                <li>Networking fundamentals</li>
                <li>Problem diagnosis and troubleshooting</li>
                <li>Customer communication</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="cloud-security">10. Cloud Security Specialist</h2>

        <p>
            Cloud Security Specialists help protect cloud infrastructure,
            applications, and sensitive data. They work with access controls,
            security configurations, monitoring, and cloud security policies.
        </p>

        <p>
            This role generally requires a solid foundation in cloud
            administration and security. Hands-on cloud projects and
            experience with identity management can help build relevant skills.
        </p>

        <div class="gst-skills">
            <strong>Skills Required:</strong>
            <ul>
                <li>Cloud platforms and architecture</li>
                <li>Identity and access management</li>
                <li>Cloud security configurations</li>
                <li>Security monitoring</li>
                <li>Risk and compliance fundamentals</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="future">Why Choose Cloud Computing and Cyber Security?</h2>

        <p>
            Cloud computing enables organizations to host applications,
            manage infrastructure, and access computing resources on demand.
            Cyber security helps protect digital systems, user accounts,
            applications, and data from unauthorized access and cyber threats.
        </p>

        <p>
            Learning both areas can help students understand how modern IT
            environments operate and how they can be managed securely.
            These skills are relevant across industries such as banking,
            healthcare, education, retail, and IT services.
        </p>

        <p>
            Career growth depends on several factors, including practical
            skills, hands-on projects, communication abilities, certifications,
            experience, and the requirements of individual employers.
        </p>

        <h2 id="skills">Essential Skills to Learn After BCA</h2>

        <p>
            Students can improve their technical foundation by following
            a structured learning path and practising regularly.
        </p>

        <h3>1. Networking Fundamentals</h3>
        <p>
            Learn IP addressing, subnetting, DNS, DHCP, routing, switching,
            and network troubleshooting. These concepts are useful in cloud
            computing, cyber security, and IT support.
        </p>

        <h3>2. Linux Administration</h3>
        <p>
            Practise Linux commands, file permissions, user management,
            services, package installation, and log inspection.
        </p>

        <h3>3. Cloud Computing</h3>
        <p>
            Start with cloud fundamentals and learn how to create virtual
            machines, configure storage, manage permissions, and monitor
            resources using AWS or Microsoft Azure.
        </p>

        <h3>4. Cyber Security Fundamentals</h3>
        <p>
            Understand authentication, access control, common vulnerabilities,
            security monitoring, incident response, and defensive security
            practices. Use authorized labs for practical learning.
        </p>

        <h3>5. Windows Server and Active Directory</h3>
        <p>
            Learn user and group management, domain fundamentals, permissions,
            policies, and basic server administration.
        </p>

        <h3>6. Practical Projects</h3>
        <p>
            Create a small virtual lab, configure a network, deploy a test
            virtual machine, practise system administration, and document
            each project. A portfolio can help demonstrate your skills
            during job applications.
        </p>

